# REDTEAM — Adversarial Auditing, 8-Dimensional Negative-Trust Verification & Veto Gate Specification
File: openOODA/practices/8d-red-team.oot

This document defines the Negative-Trust Doctrine, the 8-Dimensional Canonical Verification Matrix, the 8-Gate Blocking Veto Protocol, and the Pre-Commit Falsification Gate. All software modules, compiler passes, and pull requests in openOODA must pass adversarial certification before completion.

## 0. Negative-Trust Doctrine

1. First Principle of Negative Trust:
   Assume all unverified source code, test suites, and completion claims contain defects.
   Do not trust test reports that verify only positive execution paths.
   A passing test proves only that expected inputs produce expected outputs.
   Only hostile falsification proves that boundary conditions, resource limits, and error paths operate safely.

2. The Falsification Axiom:
   Falsify, never confirm.
   Adversarial testing actively attacks implementation assumptions.
   Every feature implementation and defect fix must begin with a hostile break-test that fails closed on existing code.

3. The Sequential Double-Run Law:
   Execute every verification probe twice in fresh, isolated processes.
   Both executions must produce identical outputs, zero warnings, and zero exit codes to prove determinism.

## 1. 8-Row Canonical Verification Matrix

Multi-agent swarms and human engineers deploy eight specialized Red Team auditors. Each auditor tests one failure axis and holds absolute blocking veto authority:

| Role | Axis | Metric | Veto Trigger |
|---|---|---|---|
| Red 1: Heuristic Hunter | e1 | Lowering AST Integrity: Phi_1 = I(Lowering(S) != Typecheck(AST(S))) | Lowering uses token bags or string matching instead of typed AST inspection. |
| Red 2: Boundary Falsifier | e2 | Dynamic Parameter Bounds: Phi_2 = I(x not in [x_min, x_max] and Result == Ok) | Validator accepts out-of-bounds inputs without returning Result::Err. |
| Red 3: Data-Path Tracer | e3 | Execution Path Wiring: Phi_3 = I(Op(x) == void or Route(k) == fallback) | Interface connects without genuine execution path or uses silent fallback route. |
| Red 4: Hermetic Jailor | e4 | Capability Isolation: Phi_4 = I(Op(x) without Cap or Path not in Sandbox) | Operation breaches capability sandbox or accesses ambient authority. |
| Red 5: Temporal Saboteur | e5 | Concurrency & Replay: Phi_5 = I(State(t_N) != Replay(t_N) or Race(tau) > 0) | Unpinned streams fail-closed, memory races occur, or replay state drifts. |
| Red 6: Drag Assassin | e6 | E-M Complexity & Heap: Phi_6 = I(T(N) > O(N log N) or Delta_Heap > 0) | AST parsing scales super-linearly, arena leaks heap memory, or SIMD stalls. |
| Red 7: Semantic Subverter | e7 | AST Mutation & Contracts: Phi_7 = I(KillRate < 0.95 or VacuousContract) | AST mutant kill rate drops below 95% or formal contract accepts vacuous proof. |
| Red 8: Byzantine Infiltrator | e8 | Cryptographic Timing & RASP: Phi_8 = I(|t_Welch| >= 4.5 or Delta_RAM != Rollback) | Dudect crypto timing leakage (|t| >= 4.5) or RASP fails autonomic rollback. |

## 2. The 8-Gate Blocking Veto Protocol

Every pull request, architectural modification, and release candidate must clear all eight sequential gates:

1. Sequential Execution Pipeline:
   Execute gates in strict order from Gate 1 (Heuristic Hunter) through Gate 8 (Byzantine Infiltrator).
   Failure at any gate immediately terminates the verification pipeline and halts release.

2. Absolute Veto Power:
   Any single Red Team auditor holds absolute blocking veto authority over a release or pull request.

3. Non-Overridable Invariant:
   Peer reviews, administrative seniority, manager override, or majority agent votes cannot override an active veto.

4. Structural Remediation Mandate:
   A vetoed defect requires structural code remediation. Test suppression, filter bypass, or dummy assertions are prohibited.

5. Full Pipeline Re-Certification:
   After code modification, all eight gates must re-execute sequentially starting from Gate 1.

## 3. Pre-Commit Falsification Gate & PR Certification

### 3.1 Pre-Modification Ingress Checklist
Before modifying product code, the engineer or autonomous agent must:
1. Milestone: Declare the active accepted RFC or the repo sprint.
2. Axiom: Identify the governing RULES.oot axiom.
3. Hostile Break-Test: Author a hostile falsification probe that reproduces the defect or proves missing capability.
4. Fail-Closed Verification: Execute the hostile probe and verify fail-closed failure on current code before writing fixes.

### 3.2 PR Victory Claim Certification Gate
Before claiming task completion or merging a pull request:
1. Execute all 8 Red Team empirical falsification test suites across challenger harnesses.
2. See `scripts/ANCHOR.oo` for expected governance runners. Those
   scripts are not shipped yet. Verify the 256-line cap by inspection.
3. Verify that all modified files contain <= 256 lines in ASD-STE100 technical English.
4. Perform sequential double-run verification in fresh processes to ensure bit-identical determinism.

## 4. Test Suite Organization & Verification Hooks

1. Challenger Test Suites Layout (`qa/`):
   - `qa/tests_challenger_1/`: Gate 1 (Heuristic Hunter) and Gate 2 (Boundary Falsifier) suites (`test_c1_*.oo`).
   - `qa/tests_challenger_2/`: Gate 3 (Data-Path Tracer) and Gate 4 (Hermetic Jailor) suites (`test_c2_*.oo`).
   - `qa/tests_challenger_3/`: Gate 5 (Temporal Saboteur) and Gate 6 (Drag Assassin) suites (`test_c3_*.oo`).
   - `qa/tests_challenger_4/`: Gate 7 (Semantic Subverter) and Gate 8 (Byzantine Infiltrator) suites (`test_c4_*.oo`).
   - `qa/prove/`: 9th Gate structural integrity probes, Dudect constant-time verifiers, and `master_release_verifier.oo`.

2. Continuous Adversarial Verification Hook:
   - See `scripts/ANCHOR.oo` for the expected governance runners.
     Those scripts are not shipped yet.

